(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology II Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has tender red lumps on both shins that appeared over a week, preceded by fever and joint aches. Examination shows bilateral tender nodules 2 to 4 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces; none is ulcerated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum","Nodular vasculitis, also called erythema induratum","Cellulitis of both lower legs","Superficial thrombophlebitis of the long saphenous vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilateral tender anterior shin nodules that do not ulcerate, after a prodrome of fever and arthralgia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 72-year-old outdoor worker has rough, scaly, erythematous papules with a sandpaper texture scattered across his balding scalp and dorsal hands. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Bowen disease","Scalp psoriasis","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sandpaper texture on chronically sun-exposed skin, the commonest precancerous lesion worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man started lamotrigine three weeks ago. He has three days of fever and sore throat, then painful dusky macules on the trunk, erosions of the lips and conjunctivae, and epidermal detachment over about 6% of his body surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme major","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — detachment under 10% with mucosal involvement, three weeks into an aromatic anticonvulsant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A neonate develops blisters over the hands and feet after routine handling and nappy changes. There is no fever and the mother is well. Family history includes a father with similar childhood blistering that healed without scars. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Junctional epidermolysis bullosa of the Herlitz subtype","Bullous pemphigoid of the neonatal period","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — localised to palms and soles, healing without scarring, and autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a two-day history of a rash on his hands and forearms. Examination shows symmetric lesions on the palms and dorsal hands, each with a dusky centre, a pale swollen ring and an outer red halo. He had a cold sore ten days ago. There is no mucosal involvement. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Secondary syphilis involving the palms and soles","Erythema multiforme minor","Stevens-Johnson syndrome in its early phase","Acute urticaria with an unusual annular pattern"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three-zone target lesions on acral surfaces after herpes simplex, with skin involvement only. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man taking allopurinol for two weeks has fever, painful erythema and flaccid bullae, with sheets of epidermis separating over an estimated 45% of his body surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Stevens-Johnson and toxic epidermal necrolysis overlap","Generalised bullous fixed drug eruption of the trunk","Autoimmune pemphigus vulgaris with widespread erosions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — detachment above 30% with allopurinol exposure. The slide names allopurinol as the commonest cause IN ASIA, alongside aromatic anticonvulsants, sulfonamides, oxicam non-steroidals and nevirapine. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with suspected dermatitis herpetiformis is to have a biopsy. Which specimen and test confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum sample for immunoglobulin A anti-endomysial antibody testing","Perilesional skin for direct immunofluorescence, seeking granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae","Lesional skin for haematoxylin and eosin, seeking subepidermal blistering with neutrophils","Small bowel mucosa on endoscopy, seeking villous atrophy of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the gold standard, and perilesional rather than lesional skin is the key detail. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man is considering botulinum toxin for axillary hyperhidrosis. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It permanently destroys the sweat glands in the treated area","It carries a risk of compensatory sweating elsewhere in over 30%","It commonly causes dry mouth and urinary retention as side effects","It is effective for three to six months, so retreatment will be needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation is specifically named in the patient education. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had polymorphous light eruption ruin the first weeks of every summer for four years. It is now February. Which is the most effective preventive strategy?","choices":["Prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B, three times weekly for five weeks in spring","Hydroxychloroquine at 200 mg twice daily started at the first eruption","A short course of oral prednisolone taken at the onset of each episode","Moderate-potency topical corticosteroid applied to the areas each spring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it induces tolerance and is named as the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an actinic keratosis on the cheek that has become indurated, tender and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin imiquimod 5% twice weekly for a sixteen-week course","Begin diclofenac 3% gel and reassess it in three months","Biopsy the lesion to exclude invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Apply liquid nitrogen with two to three freeze-thaw cycles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — induration, tenderness and bleeding are the concerning features named for progression. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with early photoaging asks what single measure will most reduce further damage. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Annual laser resurfacing, since it is the most effective option for texture","Oral antioxidant supplements taken daily through the summer months","Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or higher, applied as the last skincare step","Nightly topical retinoid, since it reverses the collagen loss already sustained"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is the single most evidence-supported intervention, at about one teaspoon for face and neck. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on amiodarone develops photosensitivity, and the cardiologist cannot substitute the drug. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antihistamines taken each morning through the summer months","Strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum SPF 50 and physical blockers","Prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy given each spring","Oral hydroxychloroquine at 200 mg twice daily as a long-term measure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — zinc oxide and titanium dioxide are named where the agent cannot be withdrawn. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a clinically typical polymorphous light eruption. Which investigation is described as mandatory before the diagnosis is accepted?","choices":["Photopatch testing to a standard series of photoallergens","A skin biopsy of a fresh papule taken within the first day","An antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La","A urine, stool and red-cell porphyrin screen sent together"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lupus must be excluded because it can present identically. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with idiopathic erythema nodosum has painful shins interfering with work. Infection has been excluded. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Rest, leg elevation, compression stockings and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug","A prolonged course of oral antibiotics directed at streptococcal infection","Long-term systemic corticosteroids continued for several months","Surgical excision of the most symptomatic nodules on each shin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — supportive care first, with potassium iodide effective for idiopathic and recurrent disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks whether treatment will cure it. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["A twelve-week course of oral antibiotics usually produces a permanent remission with no need for any further treatment","Avoiding alcohol alone will resolve the condition within several months","Laser treatment removes the underlying disease and prevents any recurrence","It is a chronic condition that therapy controls rather than eliminates, so long-term management and daily sun protection are expected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside a trigger diary and gentle non-irritating skincare. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has one facial pigmented macule that is asymmetric with an irregular border and two darker areas within it, unlike her other lesions. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical hydroquinone 4% and review in three months","Reassure and arrange routine review in twelve months","Biopsy the lesion to exclude lentigo maligna","Treat the lesion with cryotherapy for five seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — atypical or uncertain morphology on dermoscopy is the stated indication for biopsy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man has extensive blistering sunburn over his back and chest with fever, nausea and tachycardia after a day on the water. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start oral antibiotics to prevent secondary infection of the blistered skin","Admit for intravenous fluids and wound care given the systemic toxicity","Deroof the blisters in clinic and dress the base with a non-adherent layer","Discharge with oral ibuprofen and aloe vera gel, reviewing in one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — severe blistering above 20% of the body surface with systemic symptoms warrants hospitalisation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis is doing well on dapsone and asks whether she really needs to change her diet. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Dapsone alone controls the condition completely, so a change in diet is optional provided the rash stays quiet","The diet is needed only if she develops gastrointestinal symptoms in the future","A reduced-gluten diet is sufficient provided she avoids obvious sources of wheat","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet is the cornerstone and may allow dapsone to be reduced or stopped over one to two years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and hidden gluten in medications, sauces and cross-contamination should be discussed. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The parents of a child with severe Herlitz junctional epidermolysis bullosa ask what support is available. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Early palliative care integration is appropriate, alongside genetics counselling and family support resources","Gene therapy will cure the condition entirely once the child is old enough to be enrolled for treatment","The condition improves substantially through childhood with wound care alone","Genetic counselling is unnecessary since the condition arises sporadically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — goals-of-care discussion, pain optimisation and psychosocial support are all indicated in this subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had daily wheals for four months despite a standard dose of cetirizine. She is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Begin omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Begin cyclosporine with monitoring of blood pressure and renal function","Prescribe a five-day course of prednisone at 40 to 60 mg daily","Increase the non-sedating antihistamine to up to four times the standard dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — scheduled up-dosing is the next step before adding other agents. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with resolved phytophotodermatitis is distressed by persistent brown streaks on her arms. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The pigmentation fades over months, and daily sunscreen prevents it darkening further","The pigmentation is permanent and only laser resurfacing will remove it","The pigmentation indicates ongoing inflammation requiring potent steroids","The pigmentation signals a risk of melanoma at those sites in future"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is first line, with hydroquinone or azelaic acid if it persists. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is admitted with toxic epidermal necrolysis. She has a known lymphoma, a heart rate of 132, detachment of 35% of her body surface, blood urea nitrogen of 34 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 17 mEq/L and glucose of 190 mg/dL. Which score should be calculated and what does it indicate?","choices":["A SCORTEN of 7, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 6, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 4, indicating a predicted mortality of about 35%","A SCORTEN of 2, indicating a predicted mortality of about 12%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — age, malignancy, heart rate, detachment, urea and bicarbonate each score, and glucose is below threshold. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with primary axillary hyperhidrosis has not improved with nightly aluminium chloride. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy to provide permanent relief","Microwave thermolysis to destroy the axillary glands permanently","Reassurance that the condition is caused by anxiety and will settle","Glycopyrronium 2.4% cloth applied once daily, or an oral anticholinergic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical glycopyrronium is approved for axillary disease, with oral anticholinergics as second line. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with Crohn's disease has biopsy-supported pyoderma gangrenosum that continues to enlarge on high-dose prednisone. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Topical high-potency corticosteroid applied to the ulcer base","Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously","Surgical debridement of the ulcer bed followed by grafting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is approved for pyoderma gangrenosum in inflammatory bowel disease and is the biologic of choice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has more than thirty confluent actinic keratoses across his scalp and forehead. Which is the most appropriate treatment approach?","choices":["Observation with photographic monitoring and review in twelve months","Field-directed therapy, such as topical 5-fluorouracil or photodynamic therapy","Lesion-directed cryotherapy to each keratosis over several sessions","Shave excision with curettage and electrodessication of every lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — field cancerization means the surrounding normal-looking skin also carries mutations. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl with acanthosis nigricans is embarrassed and has been scrubbing her neck daily, believing it is dirt. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The change is caused by friction from clothing against the neck and will settle with softer, looser fabrics","The change is permanent and no intervention will alter its appearance at all","The change indicates an underlying cancer in most adolescent patients","The change is a metabolic warning sign rather than a hygiene problem, and 5 to 10% weight loss improves it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the hygiene misconception is specifically addressed in the teaching. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has hundreds of small papules over the trunk and limbs, present for a year and mildly itchy. Biopsy confirms granuloma annulare. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Screen for diabetes, thyroid disease and dyslipidaemia, with age-appropriate malignancy screening","Reassure that the condition is benign and arrange review in two years","Begin intralesional triamcinolone to each of the affected lesions","Begin oral terbinafine on the assumption of a widespread dermatophyte infection of the trunk and limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — generalised disease in an adult over fifty carries these associations, including lymphoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy develops target lesions and oral erosions two weeks after a persistent cough and fever. Which test should be ordered to identify the trigger?","choices":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae immunoglobulin M serology or polymerase chain reaction","Herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction from a skin swab","Antistreptolysin O titre with a throat culture for group A streptococcus","Serum immunoglobulin A anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — elevated Mycoplasma immunoglobulin M is characteristic in children and atypical presentations. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has target lesions on the palms, and the clinical picture is not clear-cut. A punch biopsy is taken with direct immunofluorescence. Which result would support erythema multiforme?","choices":["Intercellular immunoglobulin G throughout the epidermal layer","Negative direct immunofluorescence with interface dermatitis on histology","Granular immunoglobulin A within the dermal papillae on immunofluorescence","Linear immunoglobulin G along the basement membrane zone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — negativity is what distinguishes it from pemphigoid and pemphigus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has not improved on topical metronidazole after eight weeks. Skin scraping shows a heavy Demodex burden. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Azelaic acid 15% gel applied twice daily","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily","Ivermectin 1% cream","Brimonidine 0.33% gel applied each morning"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is superior to metronidazole in Demodex-associated papulopustular disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"}]};
